  "strategy": "1. Approach and rationale\n    The algorithm uses a multi-phase optimization strategy to improve the Erd\u0151s minimum overlap problem constant C. It combines global search with local refinement using basin-hopping technique which allows transitioning between different solution basins. This method is chosen for its ability to escape local minima found by common stochastic methods like simulated annealing. The strategy involves:\n    - Initial random search for diverse starting points\n    - Followed by nuclei seeding based on promising regions (Peaks and Valleys detection)\n    - Local optimization using gradient-based method with box constraints\n    The parent solution serves as a point of departure for expansion and exploration. This allows the algorithm to preserve the provided solution's quality while systematically searching for improvements through adaptive neighborhood exploration and balanced mutation rates that maintain solution feasibility.\n\n2. Mathematical specification\n    - Variables: h is a step function h:[0,2]\u2192[0,1] with integral 1. \n    - Optimization representation: Represented as h_halves of length m (n_points/2), symmetric by reflection.\n    - Objective: Minimize C = max_k \u222bh(x)(1-h(x+k))dx over all shifts k.\n    - Constraints: \n        \u222b\u2080\u00b2 h(x)dx = 1\n        0 \u2264 h(x) \u2264 1\n    - Optimization: Uses basin-hopping with:\n        - Repair mechanism to maintain integral constraint and [0,1] bounds\n        - Peaks/valleys detection for nuclei seeding\n        - WMLE mutation strategy for diversification\n        - Local gradient optimization using L-BFGS-B\n\n3. Implementation procedure\n    a) Initialization:\n        - If parent (initial_h_values) is available:\n            - n_parent = len(initial_h_values)\n        - n_points = get_closest_neighbor_length(parent_length)  # selects optimal resolution\n        - Create \u03bd-length vector \u03bd\n        - dx = 2.0 / n_points\n        - integral_target = n_points / 4\n\n    b) Objective function (evaluate_fitness):\n        - Input: half-vector h (vector of length n_points//2)\n        - Build full vector: full_h = np.concatenate([h, h[::-1]])\n        - Clip and repair to ensure bounds: repaired = repair_vector(full_h, integral_target)\n        - Compute correlation of repaired and 1-repaired\n        - Return max_correlation * dx (as C value)\n\n    c) Repair function (repair_vector):\n        - Clip to [0,1]\n        - Scale to meet integral_target\n        - Handle special cases (empty vector, zero sum)\n\n    d) Mutation functions:\n        spread_mutation, wmle_mutation, replace_element_mutation\n        spread_mutation: promotes feature spread\n        wmle_mutation: weighted mutation for diversity\n        replace_element: targeted exploration\n        Each uses dynamic scaling based on C value\n\n    e) Phase 1: Random Walk + Ancestry Maintenance\n        - Maintain a pool of recent solutions\n        - Perform global random walk with adaptive neighborhood shrinks\n        - Track diversity metrics\n\n    f) Phase 2: Peaks/Valleys nucleation from local optimizations\n        - Use soccer-pitch optimization on promising regions\n        - Identify local minima/maxima from earlier evaluations\n\n    g) Phase 3: Local Optimization\n        - Use basin-hopping with L-BFGS-B method\n        - Strict bounds [0,1] handling\n        - Repair mechanism built-in\n\n    h) Termination:\n        - Time budget exhausted\n        - No improvement in last N evaluations\n        - Max iterations per phase reached\n        - Return best-found solution\n\n4. Search choices and variations\n    - Primary variation: \n        Initial random walk \u2192 Peaks/Valleys detection \u2192 Local optimization\n    - Adaptations:\n        Mutation strategies: \n          spread_mutation (fraction, vector), \n          wmle_mutation (sigma), \n          replace_element (index, value_range)\n        Selection parameters:\n          Global_pool_size = 20\n          Mutation_probabilities = [0.6, 0.3, 0.1]\n        Objective balancing:\n          Spread mutation coefficient: 0.75\n        Constraint enforcement: \n          Repair scaling factor: 110% (n_clips-counter for more aggressive repair)\n\n5. Compute budget and robustness\n    - Time distribution:\n        Phase 1: max 30% budget (typically 30s of 100s budget)\n        Phase 2: max 50% budget\n        Phase 3: max 20% budget\n    - Evaluation efficiency:\n        Objective evaluation: O(n_points)\n        Spatial adaptation: user-based on points\n        Warm-start reuse: 75% of local optimizations\n    - Failure recovery:\n        Budget fallback: revert to parent distribution if stuck\n        Deadlock prevention: minimum mutation coverage 0.01\n        Divergence cutoff: if diversity drops below 0.05, restart with parent\n\n6. Validation and return contract\n    - Verify: all(h\u1d62 \u2208 [0,1]), np.allclose(\u222bh dx, 1) (within 1e-5)\n    - Compute C5 value using np.correlate(h, 1-h, mode='full')*dx\n    - Return best solution found (h_values, c5_bound, n_points)\n    - Re-evaluation guarantee: final solution passes same assessment before return",
